WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.380
(Eerie music)

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.580
- (Michael) What’s up, Wisecrack, Michael
here.

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:12.640
Time travel in movies has a weird history,
to say the least.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.499
Whether it’s a DeLorean juiced up to 1.21
gigawatts , a germ-filled hot tub, or Keanu

00:00:17.499 --> 00:00:18.499
Reeves:

00:00:18.499 --> 00:00:19.499
- (Keanue) Whoa!

00:00:19.499 --> 00:00:22.939
- (Michael) it’s basically always a certifiable
mind trip.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:26.839
And yet we keep returning to these stories
because they tickle our brains with one very

00:00:26.839 --> 00:00:31.730
compelling question: Do we have any free will
to change the future, or are the awful things

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.250
that happen to us inevitable?

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.840
Enter: Dark.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.861
This German Netflix series centers around
a small forest town whose residents time travel

00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:41.530
across centuries trying to change a tragic
chain of events.

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:43.160
Their ultimate goal?

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.260
Avoid an oft-discussed “apocalypse”

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.770
(Characters saying Apocalypse in German)

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.850
The question of whether or not they CAN do
anything about their impending doom is the

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.870
core of the show’s mystery.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:01.870
And make no mistake, this show will have you
up at 3AM, 100 comments deep on a Reddit thread

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:04.350
about Dark’s incestuous family tree.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:05.660
But more on that later.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:10.220
The really important question it poses is
this: Do we have the power to prevent our

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.580
own suffering?

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.400
And is it even worth trying to?

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.500
Let’s find out in this Wisecrack Edition
on The Philosophy of Dark.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.000
And, as always, spoilers ahead.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:17.960
- (Michael) And now, a quick recap.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:22.420
Dark tells the story of Winden, a rural town
facing a series of tragic but seemingly normal

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.420
events.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.120
This all quickly spirals into time travel,
accidental incest, and interdimensional disaster.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:29.900
So first, a guy kills himself.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.230
Then, a boy goes missing.

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.810
And THEN, the son of the guy who killed himself,
Jonas, starts looking for the missing boy

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.750
via a literal wormhole to the past.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:41.900
There, he learns that the missing boy has
been living in 1986 and will grow up to become

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:42.900
his Dad.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.900
Oh, and meanwhile a whole lot of other folks
are also secretly messing with time travel,

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.900
too.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:53.040
Eventually, the people of Winden - from as
far back as 1888 to as far forward as 2053

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.131
- discover that they are trapped in a perpetual,
unchangeable time loop that always ends in

00:02:57.131 --> 00:02:58.410
a horrible apocalypse.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.680
Oh yeah, and Jonas finds out that his crush,
Martha, is, in fact, his aunt.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:02.680
Yikes.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.700
So, let’s take this one season at a time.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.050
All our characters are using time travel to
try to fix or change the future.

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.140
But do they even have that power??

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.750
Or are they cursed to stay trapped in this
rainy suburban hell of a time loop.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.080
Midway through the season, Jonas and the people
of Winden discover the wormhole beneath their

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:24.190
town and attempt to “correct” or “change”
the central event - the young boy’s disappearance.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:28.110
At first, Jonas thinks he has “free will”
and - that his choices will determine his

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:29.970
fate, and the fate of the missing boy.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:34.319
But a mysterious stranger tells him that taking
the boy back to 2019 would result in the end

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:38.840
of Jonas’ own existence, because this kid
is his FATHER, and needs to grow up in the

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.599
past in order to, you know, father Jonas.

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:45.440
Examples like this are numerous - and the
results are always the same.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.000
Every time someone tries to change the past,
they’re actually just doing something that

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:51.080
by necessity has already happened.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.980
And as the characters’ attempts to change
the past are continuously thwarted, they slowly

00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:57.090
realize that they don’t have free will.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:01.240
Rather, they seem to be trapped in an endless
cycle that’s outside their control.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.230
We can understand this endless cycle with
a little help from Arthur Schopenhauer, who,

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.420
side note: was a horrible hang.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.050
According to Schopenhauer, the world is driven
by an animating force called “Will.”

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:15.200
This Will, rather than our own individual
choice, is what drives our actions.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.450
Will compels us to eat, breathe, sleep and
importantly, to try to avoid suffering.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:23.160
It’s like Will is the shitty, underpaid
busdriver, and we’re all passengers, stuck

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.240
in the back of the bus, unable to get off.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:29.300
In Dark, characters like Jonas struggle to
accept this reality as they continue to believe

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:33.160
that they are, in fact, the driver of their
own bus, able to change history with their

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:34.160
own will.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.460
In fact, one character’s determination to
change the future makes him behave so erratically

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:41.770
that he gets put in a mental institution - in
the PAST.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:47.220
It can be hard or even impossible to accept
that we don’t REALLY have control over anything

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:48.560
we do.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.870
After all, we experience life as a series
of actions and the consequences of those actions.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:57.620
So even as teen Jonas realizes that all of
his choices have been predetermined, the mysterious

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.870
stranger, who is actually Adult Jonas from
the future, keeps trying to change the fabric

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.000
of reality by destroying the Winden wormhole
completely.

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:22.680
Only problem?

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.210
Even THIS event has already happened, and
we learn that Adult Jonas’s attempt to close

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.200
the wormhole is what actually creates it in
the first place.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:34.830
‘Cause your stoner friend from college,
Ganja Greg, was RIGHT - time IS an illusion.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:38.740
The characters keep messing up their lives
because they can’t accept that time is NOT

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.720
a one-way street of sequential events.

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.370
Rather, the things they do in the present
can actually affect the past.

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:49.650
They, like we, experience time as linear,
and tend to assume that’s the reality.

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:55.160
In fact, time, according to Dark and also,
ya know, Einstein, is happening all at once,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.620
everywhere and every time.

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.070
Of course, the people of Dark might have realized
their fate sooner if they could have read

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:04.670
the first season’s opening quote by Einstein
himself: "The distinction between the past,

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:08.270
present, and future is only a stubbornly persistent
illusion."

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:12.479
By season two, our characters start to learn
that, not only are they unable to change the

00:06:12.479 --> 00:06:16.389
past, but they are ALSO completely unable
to stop TRYING to.

00:06:16.389 --> 00:06:17.389
Why?

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:21.949
You guessed it: the force of will, and in
this case, the will to stop their own suffering.

00:06:21.949 --> 00:06:25.930
Everyone in Winden is dealing with emotional
wounds: Jonas can’t get over his father’s

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.220
suicide, his mother feels rejected by the
men she loves, various people are hurt by

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:33.820
their spouse’s infidelities, and many people
die tragically.

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.670
One character, Claudia, experiments with time
travel, hoping to save her daughter, who gets

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:41.210
cancer in the future, as well as her father,
who is murdered in the past.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:45.830
And Jonas, far in the future, tries to save
Martha, his aunt slash lover, and everyone

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:47.789
else he loves from the end of the world.

00:06:47.789 --> 00:06:51.949
If constantly trying to stop bad things from
happening without succeeding sounds awful,

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:53.380
that’s because it is.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:58.190
In fact, the very will that makes us take
action to avoid suffering simultaneously drives

00:06:58.190 --> 00:06:59.770
us to further suffering.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:03.509
According to Schopenhauer, the world of daily
life actually just “is suffering” and

00:07:03.509 --> 00:07:06.930
trying to prevent that suffering just makes
us more miserable.

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.020
Like we said, not a fun hang.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:13.069
By this point, the people of Winden may have
realized that they’re not in control of

00:07:13.069 --> 00:07:14.069
anything.

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:15.330
But they’re also not sure who is.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:19.350
That’s when the show suggests that there
might be some kind of higher being pulling

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.370
all the strings.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:25.080
It introduces Adam, a God-like dude in a church-like
room who seems very bossy.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:26.930
So is Adam God?

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:27.930
Hell no!

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.310
He’s just an older and uglier version of
Jonas!

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:34.940
In a literalization of Schopenhauer’s philosophy,
young Jonas’ innocent desire to save his

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:39.860
town from suffering leads him to grow into
Adam, an ugly thing responsible for much of

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.130
his town’s suffering.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:45.389
We see this again at the climax of season
two, as both Young Jonas and Adult Jonas are

00:07:45.389 --> 00:07:47.410
trying to stop the death of Martha.

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:52.270
Young Jonas thinks she dies during the Apocalypse,
but Adult Jonas knows she dies by gunshot.

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:57.990
In a sick irony, Adult Jonas forces her into
a bunker with the very gun that ... Old Jonas

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:01.099
(aka Adam) will one day use to murder Martha.

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:02.860
Is your brain okay right now?

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:03.860
Mine’s not.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:04.860
But anyway.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:09.449
Jonas’s desire to save Martha ends up being
the very thing that sets all the pieces in

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:12.590
place to get her killed, by his own hand.

00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:17.300
The creators of Dark seem to agree about the
inevitability of certain human behaviors.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:20.539
According to the show’s writer, Jante [JAN
- T] Freise: "It always comes down to some

00:08:20.539 --> 00:08:24.820
kind of programming they had before them that
you cannot choose because you cannot let go

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:26.400
of the stuff that happened before you.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:30.650
Your past is always pushing you into the direction
you're going, while at the same time the future

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:31.789
is pulling you."

00:08:31.789 --> 00:08:34.789
But there’s still one more season of the
show!

00:08:34.789 --> 00:08:37.879
Is there any hope to be had for our time traveling
incest babies?

00:08:37.879 --> 00:08:43.219
There is, if they can learn to, like your
laconic teenage brother, just give less of

00:08:43.219 --> 00:08:44.219
a shit.

00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:45.920
We’ll let the show explain.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.730
Season three roots its conflict in the tension
between two “mirror” worlds, one light

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:53.290
and one dark, which are both fighting for
control of time travel.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:57.540
Naturally, everyone believes that they are
on the good side, even as they do very bad

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.449
things, like repeatedly kill their own family
members.

00:09:00.449 --> 00:09:05.699
So if grown-up Jonas, aka Adam, is the mastermind
of one mirror world, what’s going down on

00:09:05.699 --> 00:09:06.760
the other side?

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.860
There, in the parallel mirror world, we have
Martha, now called Eve.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.449
When mirror Martha kidnaps Jonas at the end
of season two, we cross over into this mirrored

00:09:15.449 --> 00:09:19.119
timeline where things are sort of different
but mostly the same.

00:09:19.119 --> 00:09:23.800
These mirror worlds, and the war between Adam
and Eve (aka old Jonas and old Martha), might

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.300
make us wonder: who’s on the right side
of this effed up history?

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:32.300
If there’s no free will and no architect
of fate, is there even such a thing as good

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:33.300
and evil?

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.149
Not according to your favorite party ruiner
Schopenhauer.

00:09:36.149 --> 00:09:41.029
If the world is Will, it’s an endless striving
with no end in sight, devoid of knowledge

00:09:41.029 --> 00:09:43.509
and law, entirely free and self determining.

00:09:43.509 --> 00:09:47.329
Within Schopenhauer’s vision, there is no
“God” to be comprehended.

00:09:47.329 --> 00:09:52.309
The world is inherently meaningless and therefore
beyond any ascriptions of good and evil.

00:09:52.309 --> 00:09:55.550
So neither Adam nor Eve is right or wrong.

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:59.519
As if to literalize this point, Jonas and
Martha’s interdimensional baby, a human

00:09:59.519 --> 00:10:04.189
trilogy of antichrists, seems to be single
handedly trapping everyone in this hellish

00:10:04.189 --> 00:10:05.189
timescape.

00:10:05.189 --> 00:10:09.040
In the ultimate interdimensional custody battle,
they fight over how to deal with their demon

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:13.959
baby: Martha has a maternal desire to keep
him alive, and Jonas wants him dead, hoping

00:10:13.959 --> 00:10:16.339
it will put an end to this ugly time loop.

00:10:16.339 --> 00:10:20.171
Moral of the story: maybe having a baby with
your aunt from another dimension isn’t the

00:10:20.171 --> 00:10:21.519
best idea.

00:10:21.519 --> 00:10:25.170
And in case you thought we were stretching
with all the Schopenhauer stuff, season three

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:30.239
opens with this quote by the German shitlord
himself: “Man can do what he wills, but

00:10:30.239 --> 00:10:32.170
he cannot will what he wills.”

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:36.699
And the show reaffirms this view by revealing
a few remaining secrets about the town’s

00:10:36.699 --> 00:10:42.410
closed time-loop: the apocalypse is unavoidable,
Jonas’ Dad can’t be saved no matter what.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.889
Oh, and also, a number of people end up being
their own parent and/or grandparent which

00:10:45.889 --> 00:10:49.230
would lead to the world’s weirdest 23 and
me results ever.

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:52.730
And again, everything is inevitable and infinitely
repeating.

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:56.529
So upon realizing all of this, what can Jonas
and Martha do?

00:10:56.529 --> 00:11:02.019
Well, they can commit a weird type of metaphysical
suicide by preventing the event which created

00:11:02.019 --> 00:11:04.100
each of their timelines in the first place.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:08.339
By denying their desire to “fix” their
suffering, they might be able to finally move

00:11:08.339 --> 00:11:09.970
past it -- in a way.

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:14.299
This is an extreme version of the self-denial
that Schopenhauer describes as one cure for

00:11:14.299 --> 00:11:15.670
human suffering.

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:20.060
According to Schopenhauer, this type of self-sacrifice
helps us give up the desire to find meaning

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:21.189
and purpose in our suffering.

00:11:21.189 --> 00:11:25.199
It’s sort of his cranky German version of
the moral wisdom that Buddhist monks have

00:11:25.199 --> 00:11:27.629
practiced long before Netflix was a thing.

00:11:27.629 --> 00:11:31.290
Jonas attempts to remedy his suffering by
ending his own life, and those of everyone

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:32.290
around him.

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:33.699
But this doesn’t work.

00:11:33.699 --> 00:11:38.319
Instead, the creators of Dark robbed me of
ANOTHER peaceful slumber by creating:

00:11:38.319 --> 00:11:43.910
A third dimension which precedes the timelines
of both Adam and Eve, the origin world.

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:49.019
And it turns out our mirror worlds only exist
in the first place because a distraught scientist

00:11:49.019 --> 00:11:53.220
and clockmaker in the origin world tried to
build a time machine that accidentally split

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:55.489
reality into those two timelines.

00:11:55.489 --> 00:11:59.490
And it’s only by stopping the painful event
that motivated this “mishap” that Jonas

00:11:59.490 --> 00:12:04.189
and Martha can destroy their own worlds completely
and stop the endless cycle of suffering.

00:12:04.189 --> 00:12:09.170
Unfortunately, doing so will turn them, everyone
they know, and all those incest babies into

00:12:09.170 --> 00:12:14.779
sparkly dust.

00:12:14.779 --> 00:12:24.139
But, wait.

00:12:24.139 --> 00:12:26.089
What about the will to live?

00:12:26.089 --> 00:12:30.490
According to Schopenhauer, if you were to
truly realize how hopelessly immoral human

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.889
nature is, you’d be so disgusted by the
pointless striving of Will that you’d simply

00:12:34.889 --> 00:12:35.889
deny it.

00:12:35.889 --> 00:12:40.100
Like, you’d give up the will-to-live, resign
yourself to what is, and actually find some

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.920
tranquility in doing so.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:45.910
Basically, once you realize how truly shit
everything is, you’re able to stop giving

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:48.889
a fuck and thus worry about your suffering
a lot less.

00:12:48.889 --> 00:12:53.179
And Jonas and Martha, having fully realized
all of this, peacefully make the choice to

00:12:53.179 --> 00:12:58.600
correct the origin world by saving the clockmaker’s
family in the origin world, thereby erasing

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:01.129
the tragedy that brought themselves into existence.

00:13:01.129 --> 00:13:02.769
But isn’t this sad?

00:13:02.769 --> 00:13:03.769
Not really.

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:07.429
In accepting their role in the perpetuation
of suffering across multiple generations and

00:13:07.429 --> 00:13:11.730
timelines, they’re able to do something
both moral and selfless, which is to simply

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:12.779
let go.

00:13:12.779 --> 00:13:16.749
In the show’s final scene, at a dinner party
with the only characters whose existence hasn’t

00:13:16.749 --> 00:13:32.459
been erased, one character, Hannah, describes
a dream:

00:13:32.459 --> 00:13:40.769
This 
all sounds a lot like Schopenhauer’s tranquility

00:13:40.769 --> 00:13:41.929
in self-denial.

00:13:41.929 --> 00:13:52.169
This sentiment, that the world is better off
without Winden, has been previously expressed:

00:13:52.169 --> 00:14:00.769
The 
repetition of this idea drives home the theme

00:14:00.769 --> 00:14:05.949
of the show: in Winden, to erase the world
is to erase the tragedy and suffering of existence,

00:14:05.949 --> 00:14:08.609
which seems like a total bummer from one perspective.

00:14:08.609 --> 00:14:12.959
But the show makes a final choice that actually
veers away from Schopenhauer a bit: Hannah

00:14:12.959 --> 00:14:15.480
says she’s thinking of naming her new baby
Jonas.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:19.110
And you can make of that vaguely hopeful note
what you will.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.609
Maybe it means that there’s a future somewhere
for Jonas and Martha to be together after

00:14:22.609 --> 00:14:23.609
all.

00:14:23.609 --> 00:14:26.609
Or maybe everything is shitty forever and
your Dad is your crush’s little brother

00:14:26.609 --> 00:14:31.220
and your crush is your aunt and by some weird
logic your mother is also your great-great-great-great

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:34.889
grandmother and why can’t Germans just make
a happy show!?!

00:14:34.889 --> 00:14:35.949
But what do you think Wisecrack?

00:14:35.949 --> 00:14:39.060
Is Dark the ultimate exploration of a totally
deterministic universe?

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:41.829
Or is there another way to read the ending?

00:14:41.829 --> 00:14:43.149
Let us know in the comments.

